\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The induced scalar plaquette coefficient}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{On a square torus with both periods at least six, take a real
scalar field and #{G=\{1,-1\}} in its sign representation.
Use unit weights, #{m>0}, #{N=|\Lambda|} and #{D=4+m^2}.
For the [plaquette holonomies](fdip-006N),
##{\begin{aligned}
W(U)={}&C(D,N)-\frac1{D^4}\sum_pU_p+R(U),\\
C(D,N)={}&\frac N2\log D-\frac N{D^2}-\frac{7N}{2D^4},\\
|R(U)|\le{}&\frac N{12}\frac{(4/D)^6}{1-4/D}.
\end{aligned}}
Thus the fourth-order induced Wilson coefficient is
#{\beta_{\mathrm{ind}}=(4+m^2)^{-4}>0}. The remainder is uniformly
#{O(D^{-6})} in the connection at fixed finite volume as
#{D\to\infty}.}

\proof{
\p{A torus walk of length at most five lifts to a walk in
#{\mathbb Z^2} whose displacement in each coordinate is a multiple
of the corresponding period. Each displacement has absolute value
at most five, so both must be zero. Such a walk has even length.
Consequently
##{\operatorname{tr}H_U=\operatorname{tr}H_U^3
 =\operatorname{tr}H_U^5=0.}
There are four two-step returns at each vertex, each traversing one
edge and its inverse, so #{\operatorname{tr}H_U^2=4N}.}

\p{There are thirty-six four-step returns at each vertex of
#{\mathbb Z^2}. Twelve use one axis: on either axis there are six
orderings of two positive and two negative steps. Twenty-four use
both axes: these are the permutations of the four distinct directions.
Eight of those trace elementary squares; the other sixteen retrace
edges. All twenty-eight retracing walks have holonomy one.
Each plaquette has four choices of starting vertex and two orientations,
which give the same sign #{U_p}. Hence
##{\operatorname{tr}H_U^4=28N+8\sum_pU_p.}
Insert these traces into the [closed-walk series](fdip-0071), with
#{\kappa=1/2}, and use its remainder bound with #{K=5}.}
}

\p{The exact matter factor can therefore be written
##{e^{-W(U)}=e^{-C(D,N)}
 \exp\left(\beta_{\mathrm{ind}}\sum_pU_p\right)e^{-R(U)}.}
The displayed plaquette term favors positive holonomy. Higher closed
walks remain in #{R}; the complete determinant is not replaced by a
pure plaquette action. Winding walks of length six are permitted by
the hypothesis and belong to the remainder. On an odd period such as
seven, odd winding walks also occur at higher orders; the proof only
asserts vanishing through length five.}
